{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 25,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The confidentiality exchange is straightforward, but the reviewer must understand why transfer from Virginia to Massachusetts left BSC seeking Virginia limitations rules and Orbus invoking the CDA to obtain Massachusetts rules."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the governing-law dispute requires the transferor-forum choice-of-law rule and the substantive/procedural distinction for statutes of limitations, but no knowledge of stent technology or patent law."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Both defects concern the single governing-law sentence, which can be understood without consulting the CDA’s confidentiality obligations or another document."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Lines 34–45 provide a continuous explanation of the governing-law construction, separate from the later tolling and discovery-rule rulings."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "d01 and d02 address the same phrase and construction, so the reviewer must distinguish the court’s facial-scope analysis from its supporting anti-redundancy rationale."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d01: The reviewer must understand how excluding conflicts-of-law provisions could make Massachusetts procedural limitations rules apply despite the transfer from Virginia. The court states its construction explicitly, but its significance depends on that procedural background.",
  "reason": "This case is suitable for a general litigation reviewer with some grounding in choice of law. The main learning task is understanding the default application of Virginia limitations rules after transfer and why Orbus argued that the CDA displaced that default. The operative language and the court’s construction are localized and explicit; the reviewer need not master the patent allegations or the subsequent tolling analysis. Some care is needed to recognize d01 as the scope determination and d02 as the redundancy-based reading supporting the same result, rather than treating them as unrelated contractual problems."
}
